1152:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
1154:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
1179:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
1198:| OD83 | <a id="od83"></a>The call-site allocator binding in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the call-site allocator binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | TRUST-4, MEM-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No call-site binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Under the authority decision, one allocator form per target: hosted, `with(Alloc)` and no allocator value; freestanding, an `Arena` parameter plus `with(Alloc)`. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: one form on every target, an `Allocator<A>` value plus `with(Alloc)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-allocation)) |
1210:| OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
1229:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1237:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
1239:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
1246:| OD131 | <a id="od131"></a>`union` declarations (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether the language has `union` declarations, and if so what reading a field other than the one last written means. Bears on SYN-1, TYPE-7, FFI-7 and FFI-8. The current compiler: `union U { a: Int, b: Uint }` parses (`Concrete/Frontend/Parser.lean`, as a struct whose fields share storage) and compiles; a value built as `U { a: 42 }` is linear, and `union` is neither a keyword nor in the grammar. No document specifies it. Options: (a) remove it; (b) keep it, for the foreign boundary only, with its layout and read rules specified. Austral: no unions. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-131 | see OPEN-131 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1256:| OD141 | <a id="od141"></a>More than one `main` in a program (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which function is the entry point when more than one module of a program declares `main`. Bears on STMT-7, MOD-3 and PASS-10. The current compiler: `mod a { pub fn main() -> u8 { return 7; } }` beside `mod b { pub fn main() -> u8 { return 9; } }` fails natively inside LLVM (`redefinition of global '@__concrete_argc'`) and prints 7 under `--interp`, which runs the first, also when the second `main` is the root module's; MOD-3 makes a name unique within one module only. Options: (a) only the root module's `main` is the entry point, and others are ordinary functions; (b) a program with more than one `main` is refused; (c) a `main` outside the root module is refused. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-141 | see OPEN-141 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): A `main` outside the root module is refused |
1257:| OD142 | <a id="od142"></a>`X.con` beside `X/mod.con` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which file `mod X;` loads when both `X.con` and `X/mod.con` exist. Bears on MOD-4. The current compiler: `X.con` is loaded and `X/mod.con` is ignored without a diagnostic (with `X.con` returning 7 and `X/mod.con` 9, the program exits 7 on both consumers). MOD-4 says one module is loaded from either file. Options: (a) refuse the pair; (b) `X.con` takes precedence, as the current compiler does; (c) `X/mod.con` takes precedence. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-142 | see OPEN-142 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One module layout: `X.con`, children in directory `X/`; no `mod.con` |
1262:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 |
1269:| OD154 | <a id="od154"></a>Whether the string builtins require `Alloc` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `string_concat`, `string_slice`, `string_substr`, `string_trim`, `int_to_string`, `bool_to_string`, `float_to_string`, `string_push_char`, `string_append`, `string_append_int` and `string_append_bool` require the `Alloc` capability. Bears on MEM-7, TRUST-4 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: Check gives none of these builtins a capability, and a function with no capabilities calls `string_concat` and `string_append_int` and exits 42 on both consumers (the other names were not probed). MEM-7@1 lists `string_reserve` and `append` among the builtins that require `Alloc`; `append` is accepted without `Alloc` (bug 349), and it expands into `string_append`, `string_append_int`, `string_append_bool` and `string_push_char`. MEM-7's rationale says allocation is an effect a signature shows. Options: (a) each builtin named above requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists it; (b) a stated subset of them requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists that subset; (c) only the builtins MEM-7 already lists require `Alloc`, so `string_reserve` and `append` do and the names above do not, and MEM-7 says so; (d) no string builtin requires `Alloc`, `string_reserve` and `append` included, and MEM-7 drops those two. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-154 | see OPEN-154 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allocating string builtins need `Alloc` (MEM-7; closes bug 349) |
